WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.632
This slide gives us a practical example of exception handling.

00:00:03.632 --> 00:00:07.652
In many datasets, numeric values arrive as text.

00:00:07.652 --> 00:00:13.912
Some values can be converted easily, such as forty two point five or eighteen.

00:00:13.912 --> 00:00:18.042
Other values cannot be converted, such as N A, an empty string, or None.

00:00:18.042 --> 00:00:22.297
The function to_number tries to convert a value to a float.

00:00:22.297 --> 00:00:28.457
If the conversion works, the function returns the number.

00:00:28.457 --> 00:00:31.797
If Python raises a TypeError or ValueError, the function returns None.

00:00:31.797 --> 00:00:37.187
This is useful because the program does not crash.

00:00:37.187 --> 00:00:41.122
Instead, we clearly mark invalid values as None.

00:00:41.122 --> 00:00:46.462
Then we create clean_values by applying the function to every raw value.

00:00:46.462 --> 00:00:51.872
After that, we create valid_values by keeping only values that are not None.

00:00:51.872 --> 00:00:54.827
Run the cell and inspect both lists.

00:00:54.827 --> 00:00:59.237
Notice that the invalid values are not silently treated as zero.

00:00:59.237 --> 00:01:03.857
That is important. Treating missing or invalid values as zero could change the analysis.

00:01:03.857 --> 00:01:10.379
Returning None makes the missingness visible so we can decide how to handle it.
